# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local build tooling and third-party game data. None of this belongs in the
# repository: it is either machine-local, licensed separately, or (in the case
# of The Sims data) the user's own copy of a commercial game that we must
# never redistribute.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Downloaded build helpers (nuget.exe is fetched by scripts, not committed)
.tools/

# The user's copy of The Sims. NEVER commit this - it is copyrighted
# commercial data, and Simitone requires the player to supply it themselves.
# Scoped to the Sims directory on purpose: FreeSO ships its own tracked .iff
# object/patch files, so a bare "*.iff" rule would wrongly exclude those.
Sims1/
"The Sims"/
Sims1/**/*.iff
Sims1/**/*.iff.bak
Sims1/**/*.iff.tmp

# Retail disc images of The Sims: Complete Collection, ripped to .bin/.cue
# pairs. This is 2.6 GB of verbatim Maxis/EA copyrighted content, and no
# distribution of this repository may carry it. The player supplies the game
# themselves; see LICENSE.
SimsCompleteCollectionUSA/

# EA expansion/DLC downloads (House Party, Families, Store, Skins) that some
# players drop next to the repo. Redistributing EA's retail DLC is not ours to
# grant, and none of it is needed to build or run Simitone.
mods/

# Disc-image cues. A .cue only ever describes a .bin track image, so a bare
# rule is safe and catches a rip dropped outside the folder named above.
*.cue

# Torrent files. The mods/ download that motivates this is EA retail content and
# some of it arrives as a .torrent, which is content most hosts and many
# corporate proxies refuse or quarantine. See NOTICE section 3.
*.torrent

# Binary disc images, scoped rather than global. A bare "*.bin" would also
# swallow legitimate future binary resources; the two known locations are
# listed explicitly, plus a catch-all for any Sims data directory.
SimsCompleteCollectionUSA/**/*.bin
Sims1/**/*.bin
"The Sims"/**/*.bin
GeneratedMeshes/**/*.bin

# Community 3D remesh pack - distributed separately by its own authors
FSO.Remeshes-main/
FSO.Remeshes/

# Meshes produced by generate-meshes.ps1 into its *scratch* folder, which is
# machine-local and derived from the user's own game data.
GeneratedMeshes/

# The MeshReplace mesh pack (~8 GB) under the client's Content folder is derived
# from the player's own game data by generate-meshes.ps1, so it is never tracked.
# The rest of Simitone.Client/Content (UI scripts, the string table, uigraphics,
# cursors, sounds, TS1Patch, ~4 MB) is Simitone's own source artwork: the build
# requires it (SimitoneRequiredContent in Simitone.Windows.csproj), so it IS tracked.
Client/Simitone/Simitone.Client/Content/MeshReplace/

# Manual mesh cleanup workspaces (docs/MESH_MANUAL_CLEANUP.md): per-object OBJ,
# Blender and scoring scratch, plus candidate meshes. The shipped result
# (Content/MeshReplace + cleaned-meshes.txt) lives in the ignored Content folder;
# only the authoring workspace is ignored here.
.meshcleanup/

# Local scratch
*.log
logs/

# The game writes its runtime log under Content\logs\ at the repository root, which
# is where it runs from. 'logs/' and '*.log' already cover the contents; this covers
# the directory itself, so an empty Content\logs\ does not show up as an untracked
# folder in git status and does not need a .gitkeep. (H-07.)
Content/logs/

# Committed binary dependencies inside the FreeSO subtree (C-13). There is no
# '*.dll' rule in this file and there should not be one - these four are
# referenced by HintPath from five project files and have no PackageReference, so
# NuGet knows nothing about them and there is no package to restore them from.
# They are listed as explicit exceptions so that if somebody ever adds a blanket
# binary rule for good reason, the build does not break with MSB3245 the next
# time somebody clones. Versions, hashes and licences: NOTICE, section 2.
FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.client/NVorbis.dll
FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.client/OpenTK.dll
FreeSO/TSOClient/tso.world/Mario.dll
FreeSO/TSOClient/FSO.UI/Dependencies/GOLDEngine.dll

# Operating-system and shell cruft. A .lnk is worse than noise: it points at an
# absolute path on one contributor's disk, and Windows flags any repository
# containing one as a security risk.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
*.lnk

# RIA/Silverlight projects
Generated_Code/

# Backup & report files from converting an old project file
# to a newer Visual Studio version. Backup files are not needed,
# because we have git ;-)
_UpgradeReport_Files/
Backup*/
UpgradeLog*.XML
UpgradeLog*.htm

